course: dictionary
lesson: 6549
title: A word for a shop
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A newsagent is a small shop that sells newspapers, magazines, sweets, drinks and often stamps. In the sentence «When older boys followed Sam and Freddie, they did not run into an empty alley; they walked calmly into the newsagent's.» the clues show a safe, busy place. The boys did not run into an empty alley. They walked calmly in, so the newsagent's must be a place where people can be safe.
explain: Notice the apostrophe in newsagent's. It shows that you mean the newsagent's shop, so «go to the newsagent's» means go to the shop. Ask yourself what would fit in the sentence: a shop, so newsagent means shop here.
explain: The word is mostly used in Britain, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand. In the USA, people would more likely say convenience store or newsstand, so if you meet the word in an American book, use the context and a dictionary to check.
fact: A newsagent is a small shop selling newspapers, magazines, sweets, drinks and often stamps.
fact: «Newsagent» can also mean the person who owns or runs the shop.
fact: «Go to the newsagent's» means you are going to the shop itself.
fact: In maths problems, a newsagent is often the shop where you buy items and work out the change.
fact: A paper round is a job where you deliver newspapers from the newsagent.
exercise: «When older boys followed Sam and Freddie, they did not run into an empty alley; they walked calmly into the newsagent's.» Where did the boys walk calmly? | An empty alley | *A small shop | A busy road | A school hall
exercise: In a maths problem, Ruby buys a comic at the newsagent's and gets change. What is a newsagent in this problem? | A school for printing news | *A small shop where you buy items | A bus stop | A kind of sweet
exercise: What is the nearest thing to a newsagent in the USA? | A post office | A library | *A convenience store or newsstand | A bakery
test: Mr Patel is the newsagent on our street. What does newsagent mean here? | A customer who buys sweets | A kind of newspaper | *The person who owns or runs the shop | A delivery van
test: Which item would you most likely buy at a newsagent in Britain? | A sofa | A bicycle | *A comic or a bag of sweets | A football pitch
exam: Lucy says the newsagent's is a park. Which clue shows she is wrong? | Parks have benches | Newsagents are always outdoors | *It sells newspapers, magazines and sweets, so it is a shop | Newsagents are places for playing football
